   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving the router, access points, radio channels, and packets. When you start streaming a video on your laptop, the request is sent to the internet, and the video data is broken down into small packets. These packets travel through the internet to your home network, where they are received by your router.\n\nThe router acts as a central hub, directing traffic to the appropriate devices. It connects to your internet service provider (ISP) and distributes the data packets to the devices on your network, such as your laptop. The router uses a specific radio channel to communicate with your laptop, and it selects this channel based on the available spectrum and the current network conditions.\n\nYour router is connected to an access point, which is often built into the router itself. The access point broadcasts a Wi-Fi signal that your laptop can connect to. This signal operates on a specific frequency, typically either 2.4 GHz or 5 GHz, and it uses radio waves to transmit data between the access point and your laptop.\n\nWhen your laptop connects to the Wi-Fi network, it sends and receives data in the form of packets. Each packet contains a small piece of the video data, and the laptop"
